\qc\b\fs28 NukaBoost End-User Notice and MIT License\b0\fs20\par
\qc Version 1.0.1, August 2026\par
\pard\par
\b 1. Purpose and operation\b0\par
NukaBoost is a Windows utility for keeping the computer awake during long-running tasks without keeping the display on. While protection is active, it creates and activates a temporary copy of the current Windows power scheme, changes only the sleep, lid-close, system-request, and low-battery-action settings described by the application, and uses documented Windows power requests. It does not disable critical-battery protection. The original power scheme is restored when protection stops, the application exits, or recovery succeeds.\par
\par
\b 2. Safety notice\b0\par
NukaBoost can keep a laptop running with its lid closed. Never place a laptop in a bag, sleeve, or poorly ventilated space while NukaBoost is active. Long-running workloads can cause excessive heat, rapid battery drain, thermal shutdown, data loss, or hardware damage. Keep the computer adequately ventilated and connected to reliable power when appropriate.\par
\par
\b 3. Technical limitations\b0\par
NukaBoost cannot override firmware behavior, critical-battery actions, thermal protection, explicit shutdown or sleep commands, Windows restart requirements, or administrative Group Policy and MDM restrictions. Protection is reported as active only after the required settings have been applied and read back successfully, but no software can guarantee uninterrupted operation under every hardware or operating-system condition.\par
\par
\b 4. Local data and privacy\b0\par
NukaBoost has no telemetry, advertising, user account, or application-initiated network communication. Configuration, recovery state, and diagnostic logs are stored locally under the current user's LocalAppData folder in the NukaBoost directory. These files are removed by a normal uninstall.\par
\par
